  "narrative": "All estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction, so positive signed evidence supports the original claim and negative signed evidence opposes it. The original negative-direction claims for TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss are strongly contradicted: R1 and R5 have large negative signed effects with intervals entirely below zero. Because the claimed direction was negative, these estimates cannot support acceptance of H1 or H5; instead they reveal much larger opposite-direction contrasts of approximately 0.42 and 0.83 units, respectively, both exceeding the 0.15 threshold and remaining far outside zero in the opposite direction. H12 also shows a large negative signed interaction despite being registered in the positive direction, so the original interaction claim is rejected but the apparent opposite interaction is scientifically worth follow-up. The KRAS-MYC-amplification-STK11-loss, arbitrary marker, technical-library, and matched-omics screens are not supported: their signed effects are near zero and their 95% intervals do not reach the claimed meaningful threshold. H9 has a positive point estimate but an upper confidence limit below 0.15, so it should not be accepted as a meaningful effect. H11 is an imprecise interaction screen with small subgroup cells; its interval is wide and does not provide meaningful support for the claimed negative interaction. These are associations in the supplied dataset only, with no causal interpretation and with residual potential for batch/library/correlational confounding. I did not request independent validation because no original hypothesis was accepted and validation would be more informative after proposing and testing the reversed TP53-loss and KEAP1-loss contrasts.",
  "research_notes": "Round 1 discovery screen complete. No original claim accepted. Strong opposite-direction signals were observed for TP53_loss and KEAP1_loss, and for the TP53_loss interaction with research_signature_a >= 0. Next explore should register reversed-direction claims for H1, H5, and H12 with parents H1/H5/H12 and reserve validation for the strongest reversed claim(s) after they are analyzed. Treat technical and arbitrary-marker nulls as closed unless a follow-up reveals a clear threshold effect or batch interaction. Continue screening categorical/technical context variables in later rounds, but avoid causal claims and consider whether large apparent effects could reflect dataset structure, correlated labels, or constructed assay artifacts."
}

