  "narrative": "All new estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction, so for negative-direction claims a positive signed estimate supports the claim. I apply the threshold rule conservatively: accept when both the point estimate and the interval bound nearest zero exceed 0.15 in the claimed direction; keep unresolved when the direction is supported but the threshold is not securely exceeded; reject when the claimed direction is unsupported. The TP53-KEAP1 co-context results are informative. H64 tests TP53 loss within KEAP1-loss cells: the signed estimate is +0.576 with lower bound +0.414, so the positive TP53 association persists within KEAP1-loss and is far above the 0.15 threshold. I accept and close H64. H70 tests KEAP1 loss within TP53-loss cells: the signed estimate is +0.990 with lower bound +0.809, so the strong KEAP1 positive association also persists within TP53-loss; I accept and close H70. H65 tests whether the TP53 effect is larger in KEAP1-loss than KEAP1-loss-free cells. Its signed estimate is +0.210, but the nearest-zero bound is only +0.019, so the interaction direction is supported but the result is not decisive against the 0.15 threshold. I keep H65 unresolved active. H20 is the algebraically symmetric interaction with the same numerical evidence, so I reassess it as unresolved active as well rather than leaving it rejected. The TP53 and KEAP1 main associations therefore appear robust in the opposite co-mutation contexts, while evidence for a strict TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction remains limited. The SMARCA4-loss negative-direction conditionals are uniformly strong: within TP53-loss-free cells, estimate +0.833 lower +0.779; within KEAP1-loss-free cells, +0.833 lower +0.771; within TP53-loss cells, +0.865 lower +0.644; within KEAP1-loss cells, +0.809 lower +0.678; among adenocarcinoma samples, +0.778 lower +0.701; in high-Cas9 contexts, +0.716 lower +0.614; and in low-Cas9 contexts, +0.887 lower +0.781. Every conditional lower bound is well beyond the threshold. I therefore accept and close H66, H67, H73, H74, H76, H78, and H79. These conditionals reduce the concern that the main SMARCA4 association is simply tied to co-occurring TP53 or KEAP1 alterations, adenocarcinoma subtype, or screen-quality strata. However, they are still discovery-data analyses and are not independent validation. The main SMARCA4-loss negative association remains the highest-priority unvalidated accepted predictor, so I request independent validation of H62. The PTEN-loss negative conditionals are also supportive: TP53-loss-free estimate +0.259 lower +0.169 and KEAP1-loss estimate +0.378 lower +0.193. I accept and close H80 and H81 as conditional associations. The main PTEN claim H63 still has a nearest-zero discovery bound of +0.131, slightly below the 0.15 threshold, so despite the supportive conditionals I leave H63 unresolved active. Multiplicity remains a caution: many conditional analyses were performed on the same supplied data, so accepted conditional claims should be interpreted as context-specific associations, not causal effects or generalized biological pathway findings.",
  "research_notes": "Updated supplied-dataset conclusions: TP53-loss positive main association accepted and independently validated (H13). KEAP1-loss positive main association accepted and independently validated (H14). Positive contrasts mean higher/less negative dependency_composite, i.e. weaker apparent composite dependency. SMARCA4-loss negative main association accepted but still unvalidated (H62); more negative scores mean stronger dependency, so SMARCA4-loss cases have more negative composite scores in this dataset. PTEN-loss negative main effect remains borderline/unresolved (H63) because the overall nearest-zero bound is +0.131, just below threshold, although conditionals are supportive.\n\nNew robustness findings: TP53-loss positive effect is strong within KEAP1-loss (H64 +0.576, lower +0.414) and previously accepted in KEAP1-loss-free (H16 accepted but validation threshold-marginal). KEAP1-loss positive effect is strong within TP53-loss (H70 +0.990, lower +0.809) and previously accepted in TP53-loss-free (H17). The TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction is direction-supported but not threshold-decisive: symmetric estimates +0.210, lower +0.019 (H65/R41 and H20/R17); both set unresolved active rather than accepted interactions.\n\nSMARCA4-loss negative effect is highly robust across requested discovery contexts: TP53-loss-free +0.833 lower +0.779; KEAP1-loss-free +0.832 lower +0.771; TP53-loss +0.865 lower +0.644; KEAP1-loss +0.809 lower +0.678; adenocarcinoma +0.778 lower +0.701; high Cas9 +0.716 lower +0.614; low Cas9 +0.887 lower +0.781. These conditional claims are accepted and closed. They do not constitute independent validation. H62 has now been requested for independent validation as the largest accepted unvalidated main effect.\n\nPTEN-loss: negative direction is clear in TP53-loss-free (H80 +0.259 lower +0.169) and KEAP1-loss cells (H81 +0.378 lower +0.193), but the main H63 bound is +0.131, just under threshold. Keep H63 unresolved active pending validation or further replication-relevant analysis. Do not elevate PTEN to a main accepted effect without resolving the overall threshold or independent validation.\n\nInterpretation discipline: all accepted claims are supplied-dataset associations. Conditional robustness analyses can narrow context boundaries but can also suffer multiplicity; do not treat co-context conditionals as independent replication. Constructed research signature/marker claims remain dataset-specific assay-context heterogeneity findings, not biological pathway claims. Next steps: appraise/validation response for H62; then consider PTEN main validation if budget allows and the SMARCA4 result does not absorb all priority, and continue broad driver-loss screens only after the main accepted/unvalidated predictors are better resolved.",
  "validate": "H62"
}

