  "narrative": "The new evidence resolves the two highest-priority main-predictor questions. For KEAP1 loss (H14), the independent validation estimate is +0.769 with a multiplicity-adjusted lower bound of +0.676, while discovery was +0.832 with lower bound +0.766. Both intervals lie far above the 0.15 composite-unit threshold in the claimed positive direction. I therefore accept and close KEAP1-loss positive main association. Because more negative dependency_composite means stronger apparent dependency, this means KEAP1-loss samples have less negative, i.e. weaker apparent composite dependency, in the supplied dataset. This is an independent validation of a dataset association, not a causal or biological-pathway claim.\n\nFor SMARCA4 loss (H62), the independent validation estimate is +0.817 with lower bound +0.715 in the claimed negative-direction signed scale, while discovery was +0.833 with lower bound +0.767. The raw exposed-minus-comparator contrast is therefore negative and robust: SMARCA4-loss samples have more negative dependency_composite values, i.e. stronger apparent composite dependency. Both discovery and independent validation intervals are far beyond the 0.15-unit threshold. I accept and close the SMARCA4-loss negative main association. The corresponding positive-direction hypothesis H30 remains rejected because the validation estimate is opposite to that claim.\n\nThe current supported main-predictor set is now unusually stable: TP53 loss is accepted and independently validated as associated with higher/less negative composite scores; KEAP1 loss is accepted and independently validated as associated with higher/less negative composite scores, with a larger effect; and SMARCA4 loss is accepted and independently validated as associated with lower/more negative composite scores. These are noncausal supplied-dataset associations. The conditional analyses further suggest that TP53 and KEAP1 effects persist across each other's co-mutation contexts and that SMARCA4-loss effect persists across TP53/KEAP1 contexts, subtype, and Cas9-quality strata, though the TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction itself remains direction-supported but not threshold-decisive.\n\nPTEN loss remains the main unresolved predictor. Its reversed negative claim has a strong directional signal but the overall discovery lower bound is +0.131, just below the 0.15 threshold. Conditional results in TP53-loss-free and KEAP1-loss cells exceed the threshold, but those are discovery-data subsets and do not replace an overall main-effect threshold or independent validation. The most informative next analysis is independent validation of PTEN-loss negative main effect H63. If validation crosses the threshold, elevate PTEN as a fourth main association; if validation remains below threshold or contradicts the direction, classify it as negative/insufficiently supported. After that, continue broad screening of the untested driver and marker claims, while treating constructed signature heterogeneity claims as assay-context findings rather than biological mechanism claims.",
  "research_notes": "Supplied-dataset conclusions after iteration 4 validation response:\n\nAccepted and independently validated main associations:\n1. TP53-loss positive association (H13/H1): discovery +0.423 lower +0.327; validation +0.355 lower +0.221. Positive means higher/less negative dependency_composite, i.e. weaker apparent dependency in TP53-loss cells.\n2. KEAP1-loss positive association (H14/H5): discovery +0.832 lower +0.766; validation +0.769 lower +0.676. Strong positive means higher/less negative composite dependency in KEAP1-loss cells.\n3. SMARCA4-loss negative association (H62/H30): signed negative-direction estimates +0.833 lower +0.767; validation +0.817 lower +0.715. Raw negative means lower/more negative dependency_composite, i.e. stronger apparent dependency in SMARCA4-loss cells. Positive-direction H30 remains rejected.\n\nConditionals:\n- TP53 loss remains positive in KEAP1-loss-free (H16 accepted but validation threshold-marginal: lower +0.131) and strongly positive in KEAP1-loss (H64 accepted).\n- KEAP1 loss remains positive in TP53-loss-free (H17 accepted) and very strong in TP53-loss (H70 accepted).\n- TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction remains unresolved: direction-supported but nearest-zero bound only +0.019 (H65/R41 and symmetric H20/R17).\n- SMARCA4-loss negative effect is robust across TP53-loss-free, KEAP1-loss-free, TP53-loss, KEAP1-loss, adenocarcinoma, high-Cas9, and low-Cas9 discovery contexts. Main effect now independently validated.\n- PTEN-loss overall negative association remains unresolved (H63) because main lower bound +0.131 is under threshold. Conditional PTEN negatives in TP53-loss-free (H80) and KEAP1-loss (H81) are accepted but subset-specific and discovery-only.\n\nConstructed research signature heterogeneity claims (H15/H61) are dataset-specific assay-context heterogeneity findings, not pathway claims; keep active only if validation capacity justifies it after PTEN resolution.\n\nPriority next: request or use validation of PTEN-loss negative main effect H63 because it is the key borderline predictor. Then broad screen untested predictors H52-H60 (smad4_loss, brca2_loss, nf1_loss, apc_mutation, pik3ca_mutation, braf_v600e, egfr_amplification, erbb2_amplification, msi_high). Any newly discovered large driver should be validated before being described as generalizable; all claims remain noncausal supplied-dataset associations."
}

