  "narrative": "Final synthesis closes the two due validation decisions and records the evidence-supported supplied-dataset predictor architecture.\n\nH133 is accepted and closed. The signed estimates are +0.754 discovery and +0.793 independent validation; because the claim direction is -1, the raw exposed-minus-comparator difference is negative, meaning MSI-high cells have more negative dependency scores than MSI-high-negative cells in the clean eligibility background excluding TP53-loss, KEAP1-loss, SMARCA4-loss, and PTEN-loss. Both the discovery and adjusted validation lower bounds are far above the 0.15 composite-unit threshold, so this is not merely a marginal signal. It also shows that the MSI-high association is not explained by the currently accepted co-occurring TP53, KEAP1, SMARCA4, or PTEN contexts. The claim remains an observational association in this supplied dataset, not a causal statement or a claim that MSI-high has a general biological mechanism affecting composite dependency.\n\nH154 is accepted and closed. Within rare MSI-high cells, SMARCA4-loss has signed estimates +0.658 discovery and +0.905 independent validation; direction -1 means the raw association is more negative dependency for SMARCA4-loss cells. The validation lower bound +0.482 exceeds the threshold despite strict adjusted uncertainty, so the rare-context threshold conclusion is supported. The cell sizes are small and the interval is wide, so the magnitude remains imprecise; however, the threshold conclusion is robust enough to close this active rare-context check. This refines the overall SMARCA4-loss association rather than creating a new biological claim: SMARCA4-loss remains associated with stronger composite dependency, and this pattern survives in a rare MSI-high context. It does not establish an interaction between SMARCA4-loss and MSI-high; the formal interaction tests remain unsupported or unresolved.\n\nSupported supported findings now form a dataset-specific association map for the composite dependency endpoint. TP53-loss is associated with weaker dependency, meaning less negative composite scores, with discovery and validated positive signed effects around 0.42 and 0.35 units respectively, and with robust accepted estimates in Avana-library, lineage, batch, MSI-high-free, SMARCA4-loss-free, PTEN-loss-free, and KEAP1-loss contexts. KEAP1-loss is associated with much weaker dependency, signed effects around 0.83 in discovery and 0.77 in independent validation, with robust accepted estimates across TP53-loss-free, TP53-loss, SMARCA4-loss-free, Avana and non-Avana library contexts, lineage, batch, and the rare MSI-high context. SMARCA4-loss is associated with stronger dependency, meaning more negative scores, with overall discovery effect around 0.83 and independent validation around 0.82, and accepted estimates in TP53-loss-free, KEAP1-loss-free, adult, adenocarcinoma, Avana and non-Avana library, MSI-high-free, and rare MSI-high contexts. MSI-high is associated with stronger dependency, with discovery around 0.79 and independent validation around 0.87, and accepted estimates in clean backgrounds excluding other major accepted alterations and TP53/KEAP1-free or SMARCA4-free contexts. PTEN-loss is supported as associated with stronger dependency, but specifically in TP53-loss-free and related clean TP53-loss-free backgrounds; validation in the TP53-loss-free main context is around 0.30 with lower bound about 0.17. The overall PTEN-loss effect is not accepted as threshold-validated without the TP53-loss-free conditioning, so the defensible claim is conditional, not a general overall predictor.\n\nNegative and unsupported findings are also scientifically informative. There was no threshold-level association for KRAS mutation, MYC amplification, STK11 loss, the constructed binary markers D/E/F, the overall Avana-versus-other library indicator, matched RNA-DNA availability, media serum, sex, SMAD4 loss, BRCA2 loss, NF1 loss, APC mutation, PIK3CA mutation, BRAF V600E, ERBB2 amplification, ARID1A loss, MTAP loss, RB1 loss, or the originally tested positive-direction PTEN claim. Many interaction or context-specificity hypotheses failed to support a meaningful modification: most lineage, batch, library, age, cas9 activity, ploidy, aneuploidy, hypoxia, growth-rate, screen-quality, MTAP, ARID1A, STK11, PIK3CA, and MSI interaction tests were rejected or left unresolved without supporting threshold-level modification. These are negative findings for those comparisons in this supplied dataset; they do not prove absence in other datasets.\n\nConstructed signature results should remain clearly separated from biological interpretation. TP53-loss shows strong accepted differences by constructed research_signature axes, especially signature-B contexts, but these assays have no assigned gene, pathway, or clinical role. Therefore those accepted results describe dataset-specific structure in a constructed assay space, not generalizable biology or mechanistic dependency.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty remains primarily for secondary signals. The clean-background CDKN2A-loss test gave a signed estimate +0.167 with interval +0.110 to +0.224: it excludes zero but the lower bound is below 0.15, so it is not accepted as scientifically meaningful under the pre-specified threshold. I keep H147 unresolved but reopen the thread only to evaluate a newly registered MTAP-negative refinement. This refinement asks whether the CDKN2A-loss association survives when adjacent MTAP-loss is also excluded, which may isolate CDKN2A-specific structure or reveal that the earlier near-threshold signal is diluted or confounded by co-deletion context. It should be accepted only if its signed lower bound exceeds 0.15; it can be rejected if the signed upper bound is below 0.15; otherwise it should be closed as a trivial unresolved secondary signal. Other unresolved issues include the weak EGFR-amplification signal near zero, small non-Avana TP53-loss context, and TP53-KEAP1 interaction estimates near threshold that did not survive threshold or validation support. These unresolved points do not undermine the central validated architecture.\n\nThe most informative next analysis is therefore the registered CDKN2A-loss clean and MTAP-negative refinement. Independent validation would have been preferable for that near-threshold secondary predictor, but no voluntary validation requests remain. If this refinement clears the 0.15-unit threshold, it would add a minor secondary predictor; if not, the best conclusion is that CDKN2A-loss is at most a small non-threshold-associated feature and the supported architecture should remain limited to TP53-loss, KEAP1-loss, SMARCA4-loss, MSI-high, and conditional PTEN-loss contexts. All conclusions are dataset-specific observational associations with a composite dependency endpoint, not causal effects or generally applicable biological claims.",
  "research_notes": "Final validation round closed H133 and H154. Supported dataset-specific architecture: TP53-loss and KEAP1-loss are associated with weaker composite dependency (less negative scores); SMARCA4-loss and MSI-high are associated with stronger composite dependency (more negative scores); PTEN-loss is associated with stronger dependency in TP53-loss-free and related clean TP53-free contexts. Constructed signature interactions are accepted only as dataset-specific structure. Negative/unsupported: most canonical markers, constructed markers, library/media/sex effects, and most context/interaction modifications. Unresolved: clean CDKN2A-loss near threshold; EGFR amplification weak; non-Avana TP53 context; TP53-KEAP1 interactions. Next: refine clean CDKN2A-loss by additionally excluding MTAP-loss (parent H147); accept only if signed lower bound >0.15, reject if upper bound <0.15, otherwise close. No further validation requests are available; central architecture is already sufficiently validated."
