ss all supplied cells.\n\nNegative or unsupported findings are scientifically important because they narrow the predictor map. There was no threshold-level supported association for KRAS mutation, MYC amplification, STK11 loss, constructed markers D/E/F, the overall Avana-versus-other library indicator, matched RNA-DNA availability, media serum percentage, sex, SMAD4 loss, BRCA2 loss, NF1 loss, APC mutation, PIK3CA mutation, BRAF V600E, ERBB2 amplification, ARID1A loss, MTAP loss, or RB1 loss. The original positive-direction PTEN-loss overall claim was also unsupported. Many interaction hypotheses were rejected or left unresolved: most lineage, batch, library, age, Cas9 activity, ploidy, aneuploidy, hypoxia, growth-rate, screen-quality, MTAP, ARID1A, STK11, PIK3CA, and MSI interaction tests did not yield independently supported threshold-level modifications. The H136 validation failure adds a specific negative result for the TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction in a clean MSI/SMARCA/PTEN-free background.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty remains mostly secondary. The clean-background CDKN2A-loss signal is near threshold but not accepted: H155 signed estimate +0.1532 (lower +0.0916) and related clean or high-quality refinements are close to but below the meaningful-effect lower bound. This suggests a possible small secondary association, but under the pre-specified threshold it should not be treated as an accepted scientifically meaningful predictor without stronger evidence. Small EGFR-amplification signals, non-Avana TP53-loss context estimates, and some near-threshold TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction estimates also remain unresolved or weak. H167 and H168 are useful active follow-ups if further discovery analysis is available: a high-quality clean MTAP-negative CDKN2A test and a high-quality clean PIK3CA-wild-type PTEN robustness test. These would be more informative than additional broad exploratory comparisons. If H167 clears the +0.15 lower-bound rule, CDKN2A-loss could become a minor secondary predictor; if it does not, CDKN2A-loss should remain an unresolved near-threshold signal rather than an accepted component of the final architecture.\n\nAll conclusions are observational associations in the supplied composite-dependency dataset. They do not establish causality, and they should not be interpreted as causal effects of TP53, KEAP1, SMARCA4, MSI-high, or PTEN states on biological dependency. The constructed research signatures and markers were useful structural probes but have no assigned gene, pathway, or clinical role, so accepted signature-related structure remains dataset-specific assay geometry rather than generalizable biology. The final supported supplied-dataset claim set is therefore limited to: TP53-loss and KEAP1-loss associated with weaker composite dependency; SMARCA4-loss and MSI-high associated with stronger composite dependency; and PTEN-loss associated with stronger composite dependency conditionally in TP53-loss-free/clean backgrounds, especially independent of PIK3CA mutation status. The TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction claim H136 is not supported and is rejected.",
