  "narrative": "H154 response-deadline reassessment: I reassessed the original claim for SMARCA4-loss in MSI-high-positive cells using both the original discovery result and the newly released independent validation. The discovery signed estimate for SMARCA4-loss versus intact was +0.6584 with lower bound +0.4454. The independent validation signed estimate was +0.9046 with adjusted 95% interval +0.4823 to +1.3269. Because this estimate is already signed to the claim direction, the raw exposed-minus-comparator SMARCA4-loss contrast was more negative than the SMARCA4-intact contrast, indicating stronger composite dependency in the SMARCA4-loss cells. The validation point estimate is larger than the discovery estimate and its lower bound is well above the +0.15 composite dependency-score threshold, despite the small rare-cell context. Therefore I accept H154 and close it. This supports, but does not prove, that the SMARCA4-loss stronger-dependency association is not abolished in the MSI-high-positive subset of the supplied dataset.\n\nCurrent final scientific synthesis: The supported supplied-dataset predictor architecture for dependency_composite is dominated by four major observational associations. TP53-loss is associated with weaker composite dependency, reflected by a less negative score. The accepted primary claim has signed discovery +0.4232 with lower bound +0.3268 and independent validation +0.3549 with lower bound +0.2213. KEAP1-loss is associated with much weaker composite dependency: signed discovery +0.8315 with lower bound +0.7658 and independent validation +0.7693 with lower bound +0.6763. SMARCA4-loss is associated with stronger composite dependency, reflected by more negative scores: signed overall discovery +0.8325 with lower bound +0.7667 and independent validation +0.8166 with lower bound +0.7151. MSI-high is associated with stronger composite dependency: signed overall discovery +0.7918 with lower bound +0.6763 and independent validation +0.8729 with lower bound +0.6855. These are the strongest and most repeatedly accepted findings in this dataset.\n\nPTEN-loss is supported as a conditional secondary predictor. The overall PTEN-loss main effect remains unresolved because the signed lower bound is below the threshold, even though point estimates are positive. However, PTEN-loss is accepted as associated with stronger composite dependency in TP53-loss-free and other cleaner backgrounds, including several combinations with KEAP1-loss-free, SMARCA4-loss-free, MSI-high-free, adenocarcinoma, Avana-library, and PIK3CA-wild-type restrictions. The best interpretation is that PTEN-loss-associated stronger dependency is visible mainly when dominant TP53-loss, KEAP1-loss, SMARCA4-loss, and MSI-high states are absent, and it does not appear to require PIK3CA wild-type status as a precondition, though independence from PIK3CA mutation status remains being probed by newly registered tests.\n\nThe constructed research signatures show threshold-level effect modification for TP53-loss, but this should not be over-interpreted biologically because these assays have no assigned gene, pathway, or clinical role. Accepted signature interactions for TP53-loss show signed estimates above threshold when the interaction direction is negative, including research_signature_a >= 0 and research_signature_b >= 0 contexts, and an MSI-high-free research_signature_b >= 0.7 context. These findings indicate that TP53-loss-associated weaker-dependency magnitude differs by constructed signature subgroup in this dataset, likely reflecting assay geometry or dataset structure rather than general biological pathway biology.\n\nImportant negative and unsupported findings narrow the predictor map. There is no accepted threshold-level association for KRAS mutation, MYC amplification, STK11 loss, research markers D/E/F, the overall Avana-library indicator, matched RNA-DNA availability, media serum percentage, sex, SMAD4 loss, BRCA2 loss, NF1 loss, APC mutation, PIK3CA mutation, BRAF V600E, ERBB2 amplification, ARID1A loss, MTAP loss, or RB1 loss as main effects. EGFR amplification remains unsupported in a cleaner background: H178 has signed estimate +0.0076 with signed upper bound +0.0659, below threshold. Many interaction hypotheses were rejected or left unresolved, including most lineage, batch, library, age, Cas9-activity, ploidy, aneuploidy, hypoxia, growth-rate, screen-quality, MTAP, ARID1A, STK11, PIK3CA, and MSI interaction tests.\n\nThe TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction claim H136 is now rejected. Its discovery signed estimate was +0.1158 with lower bound below +0.15, and independent validation produced a signed estimate of -0.0318 with adjusted interval -0.4035 to +0.3399. The validation point estimate is near zero and opposite to the claimed positive direction. Although the wide validation interval does not logically exclude a positive interaction in a larger dataset, the available supplied-dataset and independent-validation evidence do not support retaining that specific interaction in the final predictor map.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty is now mostly secondary. The main unresolved secondary signal is CDKN2A-loss. Several clean or high-quality CDKN2A-loss tests have point estimates near or above +0.15, but their signed lower confidence bounds do not clear the threshold. The most relevant examples are the clean all-major-driver-free test with signed estimate +0.1669 and lower bound +0.1099; clean MTAP-negative test +0.1532 with lower bound +0.0916; high-quality clean MTAP-negative +0.1864 with lower bound +0.0807; and high-quality clean MTAP-negative PIK3CA-wild-type +0.1880 with lower bound +0.0725. These are suggestive but not accepted under the conservative lower-bound decision rule. PTEN-loss in some high-quality or batch-restricted clean settings remains unresolved because of reduced precision despite directional point estimates around +0.21 to +0.23. TP53-loss among rare MSI-high-positive cells remains unresolved. Some minor signature and interaction estimates remain unresolved but are less informative than the CDKN2A and PTEN boundary questions.\n\nNext analyses registered: I registered focused follow-up comparisons rather than broad repeats. The most informative next analyses are: (1) TP53-loss among KEAP1-loss-positive, high screen_roc_auc cells, and (2) KEAP1-loss among TP53-loss-positive, high screen_roc_auc cells, to assess whether the accepted conditional TP53/KEAP1 main effects remain threshold-level in better-quality screens; (3) PTEN-loss among clean TP53-loss-free, KEAP1-loss-free, SMARCA4-loss-free, MSI-high-free, PIK3CA-mutant cells, to test whether PTEN-loss-associated stronger dependency extends to PIK3CA-mutant contexts; (4) PTEN-loss in an isolated clean PIK3CA-wild-type MTAP-negative background; (5) CDKN2A-loss in a clean PIK3CA-wild-type background, with and without MTAP-negative restriction, to determine whether the near-threshold CDKN2A-loss signal becomes threshold-level; and (6) MSI-high in KEAP1-loss-positive cells, to test whether the accepted MSI-high stronger-dependency association persists in the context of KEAP1-loss. All registered comparisons use the signed decision rule: accept only if the signed lower confidence bound exceeds +0.15, reject if the signed upper bound is below +0.15, otherwise leave unresolved. No independent validation requests remain.\n\nAll conclusions remain dataset-specific observational associations with dependency_composite. They do not establish causal effects of TP53-loss, KEAP1-loss, SMARCA4-loss, MSI-high, PTEN-loss, CDKN2A-loss, or any measured assay on dependency, and they should not be generalized beyond the supplied dataset without independent replication.",
  "research_notes": "Accepted architecture after H154 validation: TP53-loss and KEAP1-loss associated with weaker composite dependency (less negative score); SMARCA4-loss and MSI-high associated with stronger composite dependency (more negative score); PTEN-loss accepted only conditionally in cleaner TP53-loss-free/major-driver-free backgrounds, especially PIK3CA-independent main-effect contexts. H154 validation strengthens SMARCA4-loss in rare MSI-high-positive cells. H136 TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction remains rejected based on reverse/near-zero validation. CDKN2A-loss remains near-threshold but unresolved; PTEN-loss in some high-quality/batch contexts remains unresolved due precision. No validation requests remain. Registered next tests: high-quality TP53/KEAP1 conditional robustness, PTEN-loss in PIK3CA-mutant clean cells, PTEN-loss isolated PIK3CA-wt MTAP-neg, CDKN2A-loss clean PIK3CA-wt and clean PIK3CA-wt MTAP-neg, and MSI-high in KEAP1-loss cells. Decision rule remains signed lower bound >0.15 to accept, signed upper bound <0.15 to reject, otherwise unresolved."
}

