  "narrative": "I applied the prespecified signed lower-bound rule to each new discovery result: accept only when the signed lower 95% bound exceeds +0.15 composite dependency-score units in the claimed direction, reject only when the signed upper bound is below +0.15, and otherwise leave unresolved. No validation was requested because no new validation requests remain.\n\nThe new lower-ploidy direct contrasts strongly support the persistence of the four major predictor associations in the complementary ploidy context. TP53-loss in ploidy <= 2.7 was accepted: signed estimate +0.4301 with signed lower +0.3050, indicating the accepted TP53-loss association with weaker composite dependency is not confined to higher-ploidy cells. KEAP1-loss in ploidy <= 2.7 was accepted: +0.8115 with lower +0.7259. SMARCA4-loss in ploidy <= 2.7 was accepted: +0.7783 with lower +0.6870, corresponding to stronger dependency in the signed direction. MSI-high in ploidy <= 2.7 was accepted: +0.7385 with lower +0.5660, also corresponding to stronger dependency. Together with the previously accepted higher-ploidy contrasts, these results make the four-predictor architecture more context-stable within this supplied dataset. Importantly, they do not by themselves establish that ploidy modifies the effects; the relevant interaction tests have not cleared the threshold.\n\nThe two new SMARCA4-loss interaction tests remain unresolved but are scientifically weak supports for interaction. The TP53-loss interaction for SMARCA4-loss is essentially centered near zero: signed estimate +0.0321, CI -0.1956 to +0.2597. Because the upper bound remains above the practical threshold, the conservative rule says unresolved rather than reject, but the result gives little reason to believe SMARCA4-loss dependency association is materially modified by TP53-loss. The MSI-high interaction for SMARCA4-loss had signed estimate +0.1814, CI -0.0411 to +0.4039. It is close to threshold and imprecise, with the lower bound below both zero and the +0.15 threshold. It should remain unresolved and closed, not treated as evidence that SMARCA4-loss is stronger in MSI-high-positive cells, even though the direct SMARCA4-loss contrast within MSI-high-positive cells was previously accepted and independently validated.\n\nPTEN-loss remains a secondary, context-dependent predictor. The planned high-quality clean MTAP-negative test H191 produced signed +0.2022, but the lower bound was only +0.0777, so it failed the accept rule. This is not enough to claim a scientifically meaningful PTEN-loss association in that context, because the high-quality and MTAP-negative restrictions reduce effective sample size and precision; with only 40 exposed cells, the interval is wide. The lower-ploidy clean PTEN test H198 was also near threshold: +0.2336 with lower +0.1255. In contrast, PTEN-loss in clean higher-ploidy cells H199 cleared the threshold: signed +0.3007 with lower +0.1839. Thus PTEN-loss-associated stronger dependency is supported in several cleaner contexts, especially higher ploidy, but not uniformly across all lower-quality or lower-ploidy splits. I closed the active PTEN robustness line because additional post-hoc splits would add multiplicity without clear prospects for improved precision; PTEN should remain a secondary conditional finding rather than a major predictor.\n\nCDKN2A-loss remains unresolved. The higher-ploidy test gave +0.1402 with lower +0.0287, and the adenocarcinoma test gave +0.1440 with lower +0.0532. These point estimates are near the 0.15 threshold, but their lower bounds are well below it. Given the accumulation of multiple CDKN2A-loss tests with lower bounds consistently below threshold, accepting the association would be over-optimistic and vulnerable to multiplicity and selection of near-threshold contexts. I therefore close these CDKN2A investigations as unresolved rather than active. CDKN2A-loss may be a weak suggestive association in this dataset, but it is not scientifically meaningful under the specified decision standard.\n\nThe Ky-library high-quality KEAP1-loss test H202 was accepted: signed +0.8887 with lower +0.6588, despite small cell counts of 31 and 172. It adds technical robustness to the accepted KEAP1-loss association outside the dominant Avana context, though the interval is wide and should be interpreted cautiously because of limited cell size. H203, SMARCA4-loss in Ky-library cells, was not tested and remains unresolved with no evidence; I closed it as inactive because SMARCA4-loss already has substantial robustness across Avana, non-Avana, high screen quality, ploidy strata, lineage, and rare MSI-high validation, and the incremental value of this specific untested claim is low relative to multiplicity.\n\nCritical audit: the newly accepted ploidy-stratified effects are reassuring because they replicate the dominant architecture in the complementary ploidy context and clear the threshold with fairly precise intervals. The failures of the interaction tests reduce concern that the accepted main effects are being overinterpreted as effect modification. The unresolved PTEN and CDKN2A results are exactly the kind of boundary findings that require caution: some point estimates exceed the practical threshold, but uncertainty or multiple testing prevents acceptance. All conclusions remain dataset-specific observational associations on the composite dependency endpoint; they do not demonstrate causal gene effects, knockout-specific mechanisms, or generalizability to other cohorts or biological settings.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 15 supplied-dataset update. The four major predictor associations are now strongly context-supported: TP53-loss and KEAP1-loss with weaker composite dependency; SMARCA4-loss and MSI-high with stronger composite dependency. New lower-ploidy direct contrasts accepted all four: TP53-loss +0.4301 lower +0.3050; KEAP1-loss +0.8115 lower +0.7259; SMARCA4-loss +0.7783 lower +0.6870; MSI-high +0.7385 lower +0.5660. Combined with prior higher-ploidy accepted contrasts, these do not support ploidy modification, but they strengthen the claim that the architecture is stable across ploidy strata within this dataset. New technical robustness: KEAP1-loss in high-quality Ky-library cells accepted +0.8887 lower +0.6588, though cell counts are small. PTEN-loss remains secondary and conditional: accepted in clean higher-ploidy cells +0.3007 lower +0.1839, but unresolved in clean lower-ploidy +0.2336 lower +0.1255 and high-quality clean MTAP-negative +0.2022 lower +0.0777. Do not elevate PTEN to major predictor. CDKN2A-loss remains unresolved and likely weak/near-null under the threshold: high ploidy +0.1402 lower +0.0287; adenocarcinoma +0.1440 lower +0.0532. SMARCA4 interactions with TP53-loss and MSI-high remain unresolved and close to null for the first, imprecise for the second; do not claim SMARCA4 effect modification. H203 untested and closed due low incremental value. Active investigations H191-H203 are closed. Remaining evidence map: major accepted predictors TP53-loss, KEAP1-loss, SMARCA4-loss, MSI-high; secondary accepted PTEN-loss in cleaner contexts especially higher ploidy; unresolved CDKN2A-loss and some PTEN contexts; negative main effects include KRAS, MYC amplification, STK11 loss, ARID1A loss, MTAP loss, RB1 loss, SMAD4 loss, BRCA2 loss, NF1 loss, APC, PIK3CA, BRAF, ERBB2, EGFR in clean tests, research markers D/E/F, library indicator, matched omics availability, media serum percentage, and sex. Research-signature interactions are dataset-structure artifacts for TP53-loss, not biological pathway mechanisms. All conclusions are observational associations in the supplied dataset, not causal or generalizable claims."
}

