  "narrative": "I assessed the newly delivered discovery evidence with the prespecified signed-bound rule: accept only when the signed 95% lower bound exceeds +0.15 composite dependency-score units in the claimed direction; reject when the signed upper bound is below +0.15; otherwise leave unresolved. All estimates are already signed to the hypothesis direction, so they were not re-oriented.\n\nH204 is the focused clean-background PTEN-loss interaction with higher ploidy. The signed interaction estimate was +0.0788 with interval -0.0785 to +0.2362. It does not clear the +0.15 lower-bound threshold, and it also is not precise enough to reject by the upper-bound rule. I therefore left it unresolved but closed the investigation because the point estimate is small, the comparison is already multiplicity-adjusted for the overall PTEN line, and no validation request is available. This result gives no support for the stronger claim that PTEN loss is materially modified by ploidy within the clean background; it should not be used to upgrade PTEN loss from a secondary conditional predictor to a major effect modifier.\n\nThe older-adult age strata were strongly informative. H205, TP53 loss in participants with age_years >= 65, was accepted: signed estimate +0.5181, interval +0.3176 to +0.7185. This supports persistence of the accepted TP53-loss association with weaker composite dependency in this older-adult subset. H206, KEAP1 loss in age_years >= 65, was also accepted: signed estimate +0.8313, interval +0.6453 to +1.0174, supporting the accepted KEAP1-loss association with weaker composite dependency. H207, SMARCA4 loss in age_years >= 65, was accepted: signed estimate +0.7205, interval +0.5281 to +0.9129, corresponding to the claimed stronger-dependency direction. H208, MSI-high in age_years >= 65, was accepted: signed estimate +0.8762, interval +0.6006 to +1.1517, also corresponding to stronger dependency. Together these age-stratified results materially increase confidence that the four major predictor associations are not confined to younger or non-older subsets within this dataset. However, age_years is missing for a substantial fraction of the sample, and the exposed cells are relatively small, especially MSI-high with 23 exposed cases. These are therefore dataset-specific observational associations subject to selection bias and age-missingness, not causal age effects or externally generalizable demographic claims.\n\nThe biomarker interaction panel mostly did not support effect modification. H210, TP53 loss by IFN-gamma state, was unresolved: signed estimate +0.1090, interval -0.0836 to +0.3016. It is centered near null and does not support a clear IFN-gamma-mediated modification of the TP53-loss association. I closed it as unresolved because the interval still technically spans the threshold, but it should not be treated as positive evidence. H211, KEAP1 loss by 8q24 copy-number state, was rejected: signed estimate -0.0239, interval -0.1548 to +0.1069; the signed upper bound is below +0.15, so it cannot support the claimed positive interaction. H212, SMARCA4 loss by high mutation burden, was unresolved: signed estimate +0.0949, interval -0.0364 to +0.2262. The point estimate is small and the lower bound is below zero; I closed it as unresolved but gave it no weight as evidence of TMB-related modification. H213, MSI-high by 8q24 copy number, was rejected: signed estimate -0.1343, interval -0.3695 to +0.1009. The interval does not support the claimed negative-interaction direction exceeding +0.15. H214, clean-background PTEN loss by high mutation burden, was rejected: signed estimate -0.0727, interval -0.2322 to +0.0869. This argues against meaningful PTEN-loss effect modification by TMB in the clean genetic background. H215, KEAP1 loss by higher growth rate, was rejected: signed estimate -0.1222, interval -0.2703 to +0.0259. H216, SMARCA4 loss by AXL expression, was unresolved: signed estimate +0.1146, interval -0.0180 to +0.2471. Like H210 and H212, it is near null and imprecise; I closed it as unresolved but did not treat it as evidence of AXL-mediated effect modification.\n\nOverall, the new results strengthen the robustness of the four major predictor associations in older adults but do not support meaningful biomarker-based effect modification for IFN-gamma, 8q24 copy number, mutation burden, growth rate, or AXL expression in the tested predictor contexts. PTEN loss by ploidy also did not clear the interaction threshold. I therefore closed the age and interaction investigations and left no active PTEN interaction or CDKN2A follow-up. All conclusions remain within this supplied observational dataset and do not establish causality, mechanism, or generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Updated evidence map after iteration 16 appraise. Four major predictor associations are now accepted across many technical/genomic strata and also in age_years >= 65: TP53 loss weaker composite dependency; KEAP1 loss weaker composite dependency; SMARCA4 loss stronger composite dependency; MSI-high stronger composite dependency. Age results support robustness within dataset but are limited by missing age and small exposed cells, especially MSI-high. No validation requests remain. Interaction tests did not support biological or biomarker effect modification: KEAP1/8q24, MSI/8q24, PTEN/clean-TMB, and KEAP1/growth-rate interactions are rejected; TP53/IFN-gamma, SMARCA4/TMB, and SMARCA4/AXL are unresolved near null. PTEN-by-ploidy clean-background interaction H204 is unresolved and closed. PTEN loss remains secondary/conditional: accepted in some clean backgrounds, not as a robust main effect and not as an accepted effect modifier. CDKN2A remains suggestive but unsupported after multiple near-threshold tests. H209 measurement check is closed as inactive. Do not upgrade these dataset-specific associations to causal or generalizable claims."
