  "narrative": "I assessed all newly delivered discovery results using the prespecified signed-bound decision rule on the composite dependency-score scale: accept only if the signed 95% lower bound exceeds +0.15 in the claimed direction, reject only if the signed upper bound is below +0.15, and otherwise leave unresolved. All estimates were already signed to the hypothesis direction, so I did not re-orient them. No independent validation was requested because the remaining new validation-request budget is zero.\n\nMeasurement-artifact check: H209 tested whether RNA-omics availability was associated with a higher composite score. The signed estimate was -0.0275 (interval -0.1167 to +0.0616). The positive-direction claim is not supported, and the upper bound is below the 0.15 meaningfulness threshold, so I rejected H209 and closed it. This is not evidence that RNA-omics availability is exactly unrelated to the outcome, but it is evidence against a large positive measurement-availability artifact in the claimed direction within this supplied dataset.\n\nFast-proliferation robustness: The four major accepted associations persisted in the fast-growth stratum. H217 (TP53 loss, growth rate >= 0.5) had signed estimate +0.4985, interval +0.3474 to +0.6497; H218 (KEAP1 loss) +0.7627, +0.6438 to +0.8816; H219 (SMARCA4 loss, direction -1) +0.8077, +0.6887 to +0.9268; H220 (MSI-high, direction -1) +0.8460, +0.6963 to +0.9958. All signed lower bounds exceeded +0.15, so I accepted and closed these. This strengthens internal dataset-specific robustness of the four core associations among rapidly proliferating cells. However, acceptance of direct stratum effects does not establish positive effect modification by proliferation rate; formal growth-rate interaction tests were not positive for the relevant comparisons, and the direct fast-stratum acceptance only says the association is detectable in that stratum.\n\nLower screen-quality robustness: H223-H226 tested the same four major predictors among lower-quality screens (screen_roc_auc <= 0.95). H223 TP53 loss +0.3602, interval +0.1999 to +0.5205; H224 KEAP1 loss +0.8963, +0.7932 to +0.9994; H225 SMARCA4 loss +0.8409, +0.7392 to +0.9426; H226 MSI-high +0.8806, +0.6813 to +1.0799. All signed lower bounds exceeded +0.15, so I accepted and closed these. The core associations are not confined to high-quality screens within this dataset. I did not treat this as evidence that lower-quality screens introduce no bias or error; rather, it shows the main associations remain large and directionally stable in the lower-quality subset. No formal positive screen-quality interaction is implied by these direct stratum estimates.\n\nKEAP1-SMARCA4 interaction: H222 was near null (signed +0.0233, interval -0.1206 to +0.1672). The interval does not satisfy the accept rule and is not sufficiently bounded below the threshold to reject strictly under the rule because the upper bound is slightly above +0.15. I therefore marked it unresolved but closed the investigation. Given that direct KEAP1-loss effects have been accepted in both SMARCA4-loss and SMARCA4-intact backgrounds, and this interaction is centered near zero, I would not interpret H222 as evidence of meaningful KEAP1-SMARCA4 interaction on the dependency_composite endpoint.\n\nSecondary predictors: H227 CDKN2A loss in fast-growing cells was not supported: signed +0.0839, interval -0.0502 to +0.2180. The point estimate is small, the lower bound is below zero, and the comparison does not clear the 0.15-unit accept threshold. I left it unresolved under the formal rule but closed the investigation because CDKN2A has repeatedly remained precision-limited across many contexts and validation budget is exhausted. H228 PTEN loss among TP53-intact fast-growing cells gave signed estimate +0.3039, interval +0.1545 to +0.4532. This narrowly clears the signed lower-bound threshold, so I accepted and closed it. The accept is dataset-specific, context-specific, and borderline; PTEN loss remains a secondary conditional predictor rather than an unconditional major predictor, especially given the many related PTEN comparisons, boundary lower bound, and lack of remaining validation budget.\n\nOverall, this batch strengthens internal robustness of the four main dataset-specific associations: TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss associated with higher composite dependency scores (weaker composite dependency), and SMARCA4 loss and MSI-high associated with lower scores (stronger composite dependency). It also rejects a large positive RNA-omics-availability artifact in the tested direction and shows that the major associations are not confined to high-quality screens. It does not support meaningful KEAP1-SMARCA4 interaction, CDKN2A fast-growth effect, or growth/quality effect modification from these direct stratum tests. These remain observational associations in the supplied dataset, not causal effects, mechanisms, or generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 17 appraise. Validation budget is exhausted. New fast-growth and low-screen-quality strata confirm the four major dataset-specific associations: TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss -> higher dependency_composite (weaker composite dependency); SMARCA4 loss and MSI-high -> lower dependency_composite (stronger composite dependency). H209 rejects a large positive RNA-omics availability artifact. H222 KEAP1 x SMARCA4 interaction is near-null and closed unresolved. H227 CDKN2A fast growth remains precision-limited and closed unresolved. H228 PTEN loss among TP53-intact fast-growing cells is accepted but borderline (lower +0.1545), so PTEN remains a conditional secondary predictor, not a major unconditional effect. Direct stratum acceptance does not imply positive effect modification by growth rate or screen quality unless a formal interaction clears the signed-bound threshold. Registered slow-growth refinements for the four major predictors, PTEN among TP53-intact cells, and CDKN2A as follow-up. All conclusions remain observational and dataset-specific."
